    "b-index-in-the-default-epoch-cyc-19": {
      "id": "b-index-in-the-default-epoch-cyc-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "In the default epoch cycle, fixed admission and worker-slot realization are separate. A shrinking pool of admitted-but-unclaimed work is normal when the admitted set is nearly complete. Fast refresh events record in-epoch "
        },
        {
          "insert": "kg-maintain --no-tool-runners",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " work and coalescing decisions. Full refresh events record boundary knowledge maintenance after report truth is rebuilt. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pool_below_target",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " covers worker pool pressure and admitted-target exhaustion. The state reader prioritizes unhandled "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pool_below_target",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " events ahead of ordinary completion backlog so capacity-preserving replans are not delayed behind many older completion events."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-index-epoch-regression-pause-20": {
      "id": "b-index-epoch-regression-pause-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "epoch_regression_pause",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " records that one epoch regressed more report rows than the pause threshold, so the pipeline recorded its checkpoint but withheld the refill. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "epoch_cycle_error",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " records a failed epoch commit or report build. Both carry enough payload for deterministic routing or an operator to decide whether to repair, revert, or resume."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-e5-dispatch-handoff-heading": {
      "id": "b-e5-dispatch-handoff-heading",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Transactional Dispatch Handoff"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-e5-dispatch-handoff-contract": {
      "id": "b-e5-dispatch-handoff-contract",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "A dispatch transfer validates the participating state and lease revisions, persists the release and acquisition facts, and stores one immutable handoff snapshot in a single transaction. The release event carries the snapshot reference and content identity with the old holder and terminal revision; the acquisition event references that same snapshot with the new lease and accepted state revision. Consumers follow and verify the stored reference instead of reconstructing handoff state from event payload fragments."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-e5-spool-heading": {
      "id": "b-e5-spool-heading",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Transactional Spool Replay"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-e5-spool-contract": {
      "id": "b-e5-spool-contract",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Every accepted game-event transaction writes its outgoing delivery records to a durable spool in the same commit. Each delivery has a deterministic identity derived from "
        },
        {
          "insert": "event_id",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " and destination. Replay uses that identity transactionally with the consumer effect, so a crash before acknowledgement retries the same delivery and a duplicate becomes an acknowledged no-op rather than a second transition. A delivery is cleared only after acceptance is durable."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    }
